# EICAR standard antivirus test file - byte pattern
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format: PATTERN:NAME:LEVEL
#
# PATTERN is hex bytes separated by spaces. The syntax also accepts '?' for a
# wildcard nibble and '[' ']' ranges, but NOT those here - see the note at the
# bottom on why this entry is deliberately an exact byte sequence.
#
# LEVEL is one of Info, Low, Medium, High, Critical (or their numeric values
# 0, 25, 50, 75, 100). Names and numbers are equivalent; the name is used here
# to match content/hashes, because two content files disagreeing about how to
# write "Low" is how a severity gets silently downgraded.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
#
# content/hashes already carries the EICAR file's MD5, SHA-1 and SHA-256, so
# dropping the file on disk is detected by hash. This entry detects the same
# thing by CONTENT, which is a different capability and worth having on its own:
# a hash matches one exact file, while the pattern matches the EICAR string
# wherever it appears - inside an archive being written, in a larger file, in a
# script, in a buffer handed to AMSI. It also means the pattern store has a
# detection that can be asserted end to end, which is what proves the store is
# actually loaded and matching rather than merely reporting itself ready.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BYTES, AND HOW THEY WERE VERIFIED
#
# The 68-byte EICAR file, with no trailing newline:
#   X5O!P%@AP[4\PZX54(P^)7CC)7}$EICAR-STANDARD-ANTIVIRUS-TEST-FILE!$H+H*
#
# Hand-encoded hex is exactly where a silent error hides - a single wrong nibble
# produces a pattern that is syntactically perfect and matches nothing, which is
# indistinguishable from working detection. So the hex below was not typed: it
# was generated from the ASCII string, then reconstructed back from the hex and
# hashed, and the digests compared against the entries already in
# content/hashes/eicar.txt:
#
#   length   68 bytes
#   SHA-256  275a021bbfb6489e54d471899f7db9d1663fc695ec2fe2a2c4538aabf651fd0f
#   MD5      44d88612fea8a8f36de82e1278abb02f
#
# Both matched. phantom-sigbuild additionally proves the built pattern is
# matchable: it surrounds the pattern with filler bytes and requires a match at
# a non-zero offset, and refuses to produce a database if the scan finds nothing
# (exit code 7). A pattern that builds but cannot match is the failure this
# whole subsystem was in before, and it is now a hard build error.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY Low, AND WHY NOT A WILDCARD
#
# Low, for the same reason content/hashes uses Low: this is a test artefact, not
# a threat. Marking it Critical would train a user to ignore Critical.
#
# Exact bytes, no wildcards, because nothing in this build matches a wildcard or
# masked pattern - the automaton takes only exact patterns and the SIMD path
# skips everything else. A wildcard entry here would be accepted, stored,
# counted in the statistics, and never matched.
#
58 35 4F 21 50 25 40 41 50 5B 34 5C 50 5A 58 35 34 28 50 5E 29 37 43 43 29 37 7D 24 45 49 43 41 52 2D 53 54 41 4E 44 41 52 44 2D 41 4E 54 49 56 49 52 55 53 2D 54 45 53 54 2D 46 49 4C 45 21 24 48 2B 48 2A:EICAR-Test-Pattern:Low
